<!--{"title": "OpenTelemetry declarative config: from env vars to one file", "description": "OpenTelemetry's declarative configuration schema is stable. What changed, which languages support it, and a playbook for moving SDK setup from env vars to one reviewed file.", "h1": "OpenTelemetry declarative configuration: a playbook for moving from environment variables to one reviewed file", "kicker": "Cloud · Observability", "lede": "For years, configuring OpenTelemetry SDKs meant a growing list of OTEL_ environment variables, plus code for anything they couldn't express. The declarative configuration schema is now stable. It turns telemetry setup into a versioned file that platform teams can standardise, review and roll out.", "date": "2026-10-28", "datetime": "2026-10-28T04:20:00+04:00", "readtime": "7 min read", "format": "playbook", "tags": ["observability", "opentelemetry", "platform", "cloud"], "keywords": ["OpenTelemetry declarative configuration", "OTEL_CONFIG_FILE", "opentelemetry-configuration", "OpenTelemetry YAML config", "OpenTelemetry SDK configuration", "OpenTelemetry Spring Boot starter", "observability platform engineering", "telemetry configuration as code", "OpenTelemetry environment variables", "observability consultant"], "faq": [["What is OpenTelemetry declarative configuration?", "A standard data model, with a YAML file format, for configuring OpenTelemetry SDKs: resources, processors, exporters, samplers and more. A single environment variable, OTEL_CONFIG_FILE, points the SDK at the file."], ["Is it stable?", "Key parts of the specification were marked stable in March 2026, including the JSON schema released as opentelemetry-configuration 1.0.0, the YAML file format and the SDK operations to parse and create components. Language implementations stabilise on their own timelines."], ["Do environment variables still work?", "Yes. The project has said it intends to deprecate environment variables that don't interoperate well, but deprecation does not