<p>Ask a platform team how their services configure OpenTelemetry and the honest answer is usually "it depends on the service". One team sets a dozen <code>OTEL_</code> variables in a Helm chart, another wires exporters in code, a third copied a sampler setting from a blog post two years ago. The result is telemetry that differs in quality across services for no good reason, and changes that require touching every repository.</p>
<p>OpenTelemetry's declarative configuration is designed to fix that, and the core of it is now stable.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What changed</h2>
<p>In March 2026 the project marked key portions of the declarative configuration specification stable<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>:</p>
<ul>
<li>The JSON schema for the data model, released as <code>opentelemetry-configuration</code> 1.0.0<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</li>
<li>The YAML file format and the in-memory representation of the model.</li>
<li>The SDK operations to parse a file and create SDK components from it.</li>
<li>A standard environment variable, <code>OTEL_CONFIG_FILE</code>, to point the SDK at the file.</li>
</ul>
<p>At the time, implementations existed in C++, Go, Java, JavaScript and PHP, with .NET and Python under way, and the Collector used the Go implementation for its own internal telemetry<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Since then, the Java Spring Boot starter has added support from version 2.26.0, embedding the same schema in <code>application.yaml</code><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why it matters</h2>
<p>Environment variables are flat. They can name an exporter endpoint, but struggle with a pipeline: several processors, different exporters per signal, views that drop noisy attributes, a sampler with rules. Anything beyond them meant code, or for the Java agent a separate extension jar<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. The declarative schema describes the whole SDK pipeline as a tree, in the same shape the SDK runs<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>:</p>
<pre><code>file_format: "1.0"
resource:
  attributes:
    - name: service.name
      value: checkout
tracer_provider:
  processors:
    - batch:
        exporter:
          otlp_http:
            endpoint: ${OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT:-http://collector:4318/v1/traces}</code></pre>
<p>Environment variable substitution keeps the parts that genuinely vary per environment out of the file, while everything else becomes a reviewable artefact.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Telemetry configuration is platform policy. It belongs in one reviewed file per service type, not scattered across Helm values and application code.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The playbook</h2>
<h3>1. Inventory what you have</h3>
<p>List how each service configures its SDK today: environment variables, code, agent flags. You will usually find a few patterns that cover most services, and a long tail of one-offs.</p>
<h3>2. Check language support</h3>
<p>Support varies by language and by feature. The project tracks it in the specification compliance matrix and the configuration repository's language support status<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Start with the languages where support is mature in your stack.</p>
<h3>3. Write a small set of standard files</h3>
<p>Create a baseline per service type, such as web API, worker and batch job, covering resource attributes, exporters to your <a href="/insights/opentelemetry-collector-deployment-patterns/">Collector gateway</a>, batching, sampling and views. Keep secrets and endpoints as substitutions.</p>
<h3>4. Ship the file with the platform, not the app</h3>
<p>Mount it from a ConfigMap or bake it into base images, and set <code>OTEL_CONFIG_FILE</code> in your deployment templates. Teams inherit good defaults without copying settings.</p>
<h3>5. Validate in CI</h3>
<p>The model is defined by a JSON schema, so files can be validated before they ship<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Add the check to the same pipeline that enforces <a href="/insights/kubernetes-admission-policies-cel/">admission policies</a>.</p>
<h3>6. Migrate service by service</h3>
<p>Run the declarative file alongside existing settings in a non-production environment, compare the telemetry produced, then switch. Pay attention to resource attributes and sampling, where small differences change dashboards and costs.</p>
<h3>7. Plan for deprecations</h3>
<p>The project intends to deprecate environment variables that don't interoperate well with declarative configuration, while making clear that deprecation is not deletion<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. Moving now avoids a rushed migration later.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Where to be careful</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Precedence.</strong> Understand how the file interacts with remaining environment variables in each language before assuming one wins.</li>
<li><strong>Instrumentation configuration.</strong> The API for configuring instrumentation through the model was out of scope for the initial stabilisation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>; check what your language supports.</li>
<li><strong>Runtime changes.</strong> Dynamic configuration, changing telemetry without a restart, is still being designed<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Stability is per language.</strong> A stable schema doesn't mean every SDK implementation is stable yet; follow the same approach as <a href="/insights/opentelemetry-stable-by-default-kubernetes/">stable by default</a>.</li>
</ul>
</section>

